<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    Run the launcher elevated.

    The owner, 2026-08-28: "the client I would also like to start by double clicking the new login
    client as administrator."

    This is not only about honouring that. MapleStory.exe carries its own
    requireAdministrator manifest, so it raises UAC no matter who starts it. A process that
    is ALREADY elevated does not get a second prompt when it launches one - so putting the
    elevation here turns two prompts into one, at the moment a person is looking at the
    window and expecting it.

    docs/launcher.md argued the other way when it was written: "a launcher that demands admin
    every time is the opposite of professional", and suggested elevating only to add the
    firewall rule. That reasoning was about the firewall rule, which is now a one-time step in
    install.ps1. The client's own manifest is the part that could not be designed away.
  -->
  <!--
    THE ELEVATION IS NOT DECLARED HERE. It is `/MANIFESTUAC:level='requireAdministrator'` in
    build.rs, and that is not a style choice - putting a <trustInfo> block in this file fails
    the build:

        manifest authoring error c1010001: Values of attribute "level" not equal in
        different manifest snippets
        LINK : fatal error LNK1327: failure during running mt.exe

    The MSVC linker always generates its own UAC fragment (`asInvoker` by default) and then
    merges it with this file. Two fragments naming different levels is an error rather than
    an override, so exactly one of them may say it, and the linker's own switch is the one
    that wins cleanly. This file supplies what the switch cannot: DPI and OS compatibility.
  -->

  <!-- Per-monitor DPI, so the window is not blurry on a scaled display. -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">permonitorv2,permonitor</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

  <!-- Ask for the modern common controls and declare Windows 10/11 support, so the UAC
       shield and the title bar look like every other application on the machine. -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
